# 2. ACCEPT NORMAL HUMAN USERNAMES AND NAMES

The current application uses a strict ASCII-only username pattern:

```typescript
/^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{2,49}$/
```

The live database also has a matching `profiles_username_format` constraint.

These rules reject many ordinary school-family names.

The new implementation must support normal human names and culturally appropriate text.

Examples that should be considered valid for a human-readable sign-in name when unique:

```text
Mohamed
Mohamed Ben Ali
Sarra Ben Amor
Fatma Zahra
Élodie
Amélie Martin
Ahmed_2026
Sarra-Ben-Amor
محمد
محمد علي
فاطمة الزهراء
أمينة بن عمر
```

These are representative examples, not a complete language whitelist.

### Mandatory username behavior

1. Accept Unicode letters, combining marks, appropriate numbers and ordinary separators.
2. Support Arabic script and accented Latin characters.
3. Permit spaces inside human-readable usernames.
4. Trim accidental leading/trailing spaces.
5. Handle repeated whitespace consistently.
6. Use a defined, tested Unicode-normalization policy.
7. Preserve the correct visible spelling and accents in the display value.
8. Apply reliable uniqueness rules to the canonical username key.
9. Treat capitalization variations consistently under the approved normalization policy.
10. Reject only genuinely unsafe, ambiguous or unsupported input, with understandable feedback.

Do not arbitrarily restrict usernames to English characters.

Do not strip Arabic letters or French accents.

Do not silently transliterate a person's name into something they cannot recognize.

Do not turn a full name into a globally unique login identifier without checking ambiguity.

Protect against control characters, invisible-character abuse, bidirectional-control spoofing, excessive input length, normalization collisions and injection attempts.

If a username resembles a phone number or email address, the authentication resolver must not silently select the wrong user. Use an explicit, accessible identifier-type choice when automatic resolution is ambiguous, or a safe collision-prevention policy proven by tests.

Do not create account-enumeration vulnerabilities.

### Full-name handling

A person's real full name is not the same as a username.

Full names must support spaces, accents, Arabic and other normal scripts, including families sharing the same surname.

Two people can have the same full name without being the same user.

Do not impose username uniqueness rules on full names.

